WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:09.360
On June 18, 1976 in Rancho Cordova, just east&nbsp;
of Sacramento in California, a 23-year-old woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.320
was alone in her home asleep. She shared&nbsp;
the house with her father who was away. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.080
Around 4 am, she awoke with a start.&nbsp;
Someone was standing by her bedroom door.  
&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.280
And I looked up and I thought it was my&nbsp;
dad, at first. He drives weird hours and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.720
he might have come in early cause it was early&nbsp;
in the morning. And nope, it wasn’t my dad. 
&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.760
The stranger was naked from the waist down.
 
So, he came in, he had a ski mask on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.600
And jumped on the bed and had a knife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.280
I don’t exactly remember what he said.&nbsp;
Something to the effect of “Don’t scream”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.880
And tied my hands behind my back. I just&nbsp;
remember feeling extremely threatened. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.520
He raped her. After he left, she managed to&nbsp;
call 911 while her hands were tightly bound.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.760
Joseph James DeAngelo started by&nbsp;
targeting women who were home alone.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.680
Later, he tortured them even&nbsp;
if their partners were home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.000
putting dishes on the backs of men while&nbsp;
he assaulted their girlfriends and wives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.040
telling them if any dishes&nbsp;
broke, he’d kill them both. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:26.640
From 1976 to 1986, he burglarized more than 120&nbsp;
homes, raped more than 50 women, and murdered 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.800
people in the state of California. 
 
A crime writer dubbed him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.520
“Golden State Killer”
 
He had other nicknames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.000
depending on where he was active. 
 
He was known as the East Area Rapist.
 &nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.520
The Original Night Stalker 
 
And the Visalia Ransacker. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.760
Police originally thought they&nbsp;
were different culprits…however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:53.120
there was one similar trait. The survivors&nbsp;
remarked the perpetrator had a tiny you-know-what.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.640
DNA evidence confirmed they&nbsp;
were the same person.
 &nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.342
But DeAngelo was not on any police suspect list.

00:02:00.342 --> 00:02:03.062
He hid in plain sight, living in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.062 --> 00:02:07.142
community he terrorized - taking&nbsp;
the innocence out of America.

00:02:07.142 --> 00:02:10.982
The 1970s was a time when no one&nbsp;
locked their doors during the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.982 --> 00:02:13.462
and parents let their kids play outside.

00:02:13.462 --> 00:02:17.222
That changed when DeAngelo&nbsp;
started terrorizing California. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:17.222 --> 00:02:19.942
He carefully studied his targets beforehand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.942 --> 00:02:22.742
Traits he may have picked&nbsp;
up as a police officer. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:23.462 --> 00:02:28.582
He was a cop when he first turned to crime&nbsp;
which likely helped him avoid suspicion.
 &nbsp;

00:02:28.582 --> 00:02:33.782
He knew enough to wear gloves. He was careful. 
 
But what he could not have anticipated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.782 --> 00:02:37.142
was the advent of DNA. 
 
The answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.702 --> 00:02:43.782
was and was always going to be in the DNA.
 
When he first started breaking into homes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.782 --> 00:02:49.302
the seventies by prying open a window or door,&nbsp;
detectives didn’t know anything about DNA. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:55.622
That would change in 1984 when British geneticist&nbsp;
Sir Alec Jeffreys had a eureka moment. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:55.622 --> 00:03:00.662
He realized you could identify an individual by&nbsp;
looking at unique patterns in their DNA through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.662 --> 00:03:05.062
a method called DNA fingerprinting. 
 
This was ground-breaking considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.062 --> 00:03:12.182
the DNA of any two people is 99.9% the same. 
 
DNA fingerprinting looks at the differences in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.182 --> 00:03:19.382
the .1% through small samples of DNA from skin&nbsp;
cells, hair roots, blood, saliva, and semen.
 &nbsp;

00:03:19.942 --> 00:03:24.902
DNA fingerprinting focuses on the regions&nbsp;
containing short repeated sequences of DNA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.902 --> 00:03:29.142
called Short Tandem Repeats (STRs).   
 
The number of repeats varies widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:32.982
from person to person. This is&nbsp;
unique to you. Like a fingerprint.
&nbsp;

00:03:32.982 --> 00:03:37.782
The sample of DNA is put into a&nbsp;
gel plate, then, using electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.782 --> 00:03:43.542
those short repeated sequences of DNA start&nbsp;
to migrate…forming a unique pattern of bars.

00:03:43.542 --> 00:03:46.902
The suspect with the pattern that&nbsp;
matches the DNA from the crime scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.902 --> 00:03:49.542
is guilty. In this case, suspect 1. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:50.102 --> 00:03:55.142
DNA fingerprinting became the forensic tool&nbsp;
used to hunt the Golden State Killer. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:56.182 --> 00:04:01.782
He had left his DNA at the various crime scenes. 
 
He tended to snack from his victims’ fridges. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:01.782 --> 00:04:05.862
Rummaged through their belongings. 
 
Investigators also collected semen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.862 --> 00:04:08.262
found on the victims’ bodies.
 
Unfortunately, even though the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.262 --> 00:04:12.102
had samples of the killer’s DNA, they&nbsp;
had nothing to compare it to because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.102 --> 00:04:17.862
the killer wasn’t in any criminal database.&nbsp;
They had no idea to whom the DNA belonged. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:17.862 --> 00:04:24.662
We all knew we were looking for a needle&nbsp;
in a haystack. But the needle was there.
 &nbsp;

00:04:25.222 --> 00:04:29.222
In the end, they found the needle in&nbsp;
the haystack from an unexpected source:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.782 --> 00:04:33.782
a genealogy website that millions of&nbsp;
people used to trace their family tree.  
 &nbsp;

00:04:34.342 --> 00:04:37.302
Investigators used semen&nbsp;
from a rape kit to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.302 --> 00:04:42.662
a DNA profile of the Golden State Killer. 
 
They then uploaded it to GEDmatch - a free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.662 --> 00:04:46.342
public site where users can upload&nbsp;
and analyze their DNA sequences. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:47.062 --> 00:04:51.062
Authorities also uploaded the DNA&nbsp;
profile onto two private sites:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.062 --> 00:04:55.942
FamilyTreeDNA and MyHeritage. 
 
According to the Los Angeles Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.942 --> 00:05:00.102
it was MyHeritage that identified a close&nbsp;
relative of the Golden State Killer. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:00.662 --> 00:05:05.622
That relative presumably had mailed in a swab&nbsp;
kit to learn about their ethnic background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.622 --> 00:05:09.222
find long-lost relatives, or&nbsp;
discover hereditary diseases. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:09.222 --> 00:05:14.182
By tracking down the close relative, investigators&nbsp;
managed to narrow down their list of suspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.182 --> 00:05:19.142
and in 2018, they focused on&nbsp;
72-year-old Joseph James DeAngelo.
 &nbsp;

00:05:20.262 --> 00:05:23.782
Was this the man who had eluded&nbsp;
the police for 42 years? 
&nbsp;

00:05:24.342 --> 00:05:28.902
The Golden State Killer liked to call the cops&nbsp;
and threaten them. He also taunted his victims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.902 --> 00:05:37.382
over the phone before and after attacks. 
 
 
The FBI released this creepy 1977 call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.382 --> 00:05:47.142
from DeAngelo to one of his&nbsp;
victims who survived his attack. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:15.193
Hello?
Hello?&nbsp;

00:06:15.193 --> 00:06:15.567
Caller: [Heavy breathing]
I'm gonna kill you.&nbsp;

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:15.822
Gonna kill you.
Gonna kill you.&nbsp;

00:06:15.822 --> 00:06:16.025
Gonna kill you.
B--ch.&nbsp;

00:06:16.025 --> 00:06:18.182
B--ch. 
 
At the time of the phone call, DeAngelo had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.182 --> 00:06:23.942
already broken into numerous homes and brutally&nbsp;
raped women, but he had not yet started to kill.
 &nbsp;

00:06:24.582 --> 00:06:31.462
That would change on February 2nd, 1978. 
 
Brian and Katie Maggiore were out walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.462 --> 00:06:34.822
their dog at night near their&nbsp;
home in the Sacramento area.
&nbsp;

00:06:34.822 --> 00:06:38.182
For some reason, they ended up in a&nbsp;
backyard, where they were confronted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.182 --> 00:06:43.382
a man wearing a ski mask. The couple may have&nbsp;
interrupted one of his crimes in progress. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:43.382 --> 00:06:48.982
Brian was shot. Katie ran away, screaming&nbsp;
for help, but DeAngelo caught up with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.982 --> 00:06:55.302
and killed her, too. Katie was 20 years old.&nbsp;
Brian, who was an air force sergeant, was 21. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:56.502 --> 00:06:59.542
The level of violence intensified&nbsp;
in the following years.

00:07:00.182 --> 00:07:07.862
On July 27, 1981, Cheri Domingo and Greg Sanchez&nbsp;
were murdered in a home near Santa Barbara He shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.862 --> 00:07:13.222
Greg but he didn't die. He then beat Greg to death&nbsp;
before raping Cheri and beating her to death.

00:07:13.782 --> 00:07:16.902
Then his attacks suddenly came to a halt. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:17.622 --> 00:07:22.502
No one knows why. But it did coincide with the&nbsp;
birth of his first daughter that same year. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:23.382 --> 00:07:29.622
Five years later, it started again. 
 
On May 4, 1986, 18-year-old Janelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.622 --> 00:07:33.702
Cruz was home alone. Her mom and&nbsp;
stepdad were away on vacation. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:33.702 --> 00:07:37.942
A friend had come over that night. They thought&nbsp;
they heard a noise in the garage but thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.942 --> 00:07:43.862
nothing of it. Her friend left soon after. 
 
Janelle’s body was found the next day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:48.822
She had been raped and beaten to death. She&nbsp;
was the Golden State Killer’s final victim. 
&nbsp;

00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:54.982
Although forensics managed to collect the killers’&nbsp;
DNA, this was still the early days of DNA testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.982 --> 00:07:59.062
so testing capacity was limited, and the&nbsp;
sample remained in storage for years.
 &nbsp;

00:07:59.702 --> 00:08:05.622
It wasn’t until 2001 that DNA tests finally&nbsp;
linked all the crime scenes to one person.
&nbsp;

00:08:06.262 --> 00:08:10.422
When authorities zeroed in on DeAngelo&nbsp;
thanks to the ancestry website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.422 --> 00:08:15.382
they went to work gathering his DNA to see if it&nbsp;
was indeed a match for the Golden State Killer. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:16.102 --> 00:08:19.862
They collected DNA from the handle&nbsp;
of his car door while he shopped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.862 --> 00:08:26.902
and some from a discarded tissue in his trash. 
 
And bingo – his DNA was a match for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.902 --> 00:08:30.182
Golden State Killer. 
 
After all this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.685 --> 00:08:36.262
DeAngelo lived free in a nice Sacramento suburb. 
 
He went on to become a father and grandfather.

00:08:36.822 --> 00:08:41.222
When police arrested DeAngelo, he had recently&nbsp;
retired from a career as a truck mechanic. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:41.942 --> 00:08:47.462
He had been fired from his job as an officer early&nbsp;
into his attacks after getting caught shoplifting.

00:08:48.273 --> 00:08:53.382
DeAngelo was living in Citrus Heights, California&nbsp;
- a short drive from where he attacked his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.382 --> 00:08:57.542
victim in 1976. 
 
For 42 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.542 --> 00:09:00.502
she had longed for the day he’d be caught. 
 
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7expnUQ-hQ4
 &nbsp;

00:09:00.502 --> 00:09:06.022
But I know it’s not good to hold hate in your&nbsp;
heart and all that, and I don’t think I do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.502 --> 00:09:12.982
I’m just a lot more careful.&nbsp;
I don’t want him to be dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.982 --> 00:09:17.382
because I think that would be the easy way&nbsp;
out of him. And I don’t want that. I’d rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.382 --> 00:09:24.022
you know, him be tried and convicted and&nbsp;
spend the rest of your life in prison. 
 

00:09:25.233 --> 00:09:28.582
DeAngelo was charged with 12&nbsp;
counts of first-degree murder. 
&nbsp;

00:09:29.222 --> 00:09:34.582
Prosecutors then added a 13th murder charge for&nbsp;
the killing of a college professor who was shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.582 --> 00:09:39.165
while trying to stop the kidnapping of his teenage&nbsp;
daughter in 1975. The case had been unsolved. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:39.165 --> 00:09:45.382
DeAngelo pled guilty to all charges. 
 
Before sentencing, he rose from his wheelchair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.382 --> 00:09:49.222
took off his mask, and said to the&nbsp;
courtroom filled with survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.222 --> 00:09:53.702
and the family members of victims: 
 
“I listened to all your statements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.702 --> 00:09:57.622
each one of them, and I’m truly&nbsp;
sorry for everyone I’ve hurt.”
 &nbsp;

00:09:58.742 --> 00:10:05.542
On August 21, 2020, at the age of 74,&nbsp;
DeAngelo was sentenced to life in prison. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:06.262 --> 00:10:10.742
He will die behind bars.
 
It has only been in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.742 --> 00:10:15.942
past few years that millions of people have&nbsp;
submitted their DNA to genealogy websites. 
 

00:10:15.942 --> 00:10:19.622
This has led to debate about whether&nbsp;
law enforcement should have the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.622 --> 00:10:23.782
to use information consumers hand&nbsp;
over to genetic testing companies.
 &nbsp;

00:10:23.782 --> 00:10:28.262
The president of FamilyTreeDNA apologized&nbsp;
to customers for failing to disclose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.262 --> 00:10:32.022
that it was sharing data with federal&nbsp;
investigators working to solve crimes. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:32.662 --> 00:10:36.182
There were no complaints from&nbsp;
DeAngelo's victims or their loved ones.
 &nbsp;

00:10:36.742 --> 00:10:41.222
Without the help of modern technology,&nbsp;
the Golden State Killer would likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.222 --> 00:10:44.662
never have been caught. 
 
Innovative technologies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.662 --> 00:10:48.342
helped catch criminals while also&nbsp;
freeing the wrongfully convicted.

